{"labs": [{"id": 0, "type": "scholar", "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0}, {"id": 1, "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0", "image": "engenn9le05ob01re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "Okunoshima  island, I was shocked to find out, has had a dark past. You can arrange to visit this small Japanese island that's only a short trip from the mainland. A place, that for many years, did not in fact, exist.<br/><br/>Between 1929 and 1945 the island was home to the Japanese army and was used to house a secret poison gas factory. Because of this, the island was removed from Japanese maps. It just appeared to disappear!", "id": 1, "time": "100", "image": "engenn9le05ob01re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "The army chose to use Okunoshima because it had a discreet location and is far from from civilian populations. They decided to bring rabbits onto the island so that they could test the effects of the poison gas on them.<br/><br/>", "id": 2, "time": "25800", "image": "engenn9le05ob01re2img02.jpg"}, {"text": "Today the island has changed. The poison gas factory has closed down and has been replaced by a museum. The military lookout posts that were on the island have fallen into disrepair. However, the descendants of the the unfortunate test rabbits are still there, and they have grown in number.<br/><br/>These days, the island has a hotel, a small camping ground and a golf course. Visitors to the island can enjoy swimming in the clean water that surrounds the island. But all that most day-visitors think about doing is feeding the feral rabbits. After all, they are the real attraction.<br/><br/>Imagine roaming wooded areas and walking the paths, being chased by hundreds of wild bunnies! You can't help thinking how cute they are, even when they are swarming all over you trying to get to that food you brought along!", "id": 3, "time": "36200", "image": "engenn9le05ob01re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": 